170	<p>Twenty-nine items, none of them read. The page holds only what you have not read. At 07:45 UTC on 8 November, your comment on change 38 marked changes 37 and 38 read, so they left, and the page starts at change 39. No comment marks a later item read.</p>
171	<p>Climb batch 27 landed on <code>main</code>, and its landing made some sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 16:18 and 16:28 UTC on 7 November. Changes 39 to 41 come from the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: the update rewrote three of its sections for the landing, its judge said to install all three, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 42 to 47 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>tests-writing.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with what the batch changed in walk, the library and the code generator. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
172	<p>Change 48 came after the landing, at your word in chat. The skill writer made it in two commits to <code>committing.md</code>, at 16:42 and 16:48 UTC on 7 November, the second with the fixes after its cold read.</p>
173	<p>On 8 November, your comments on changes 37 and 39 led to two more commits. At your word of 01:07 UTC, the skill writer made the fixes in one commit at 01:09 UTC. A cold read then found passages that would send a worker wrong, and the skill writer fixed them in a second commit at 02:06 UTC. The two commits changed three passages. One is the passage of change 39, which now shows these changes too, with a second reason, &ldquo;Why, 8 November&rdquo;. Another, in <code>SKILL.md</code>, is change 49. The third is the passage of change 37, which you have since read.</p>
174	<p>Climb batch 28 landed on <code>main</code> on 8 November, and its landing made more sentences of the skill false or incomplete. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 11:23, 11:27 and 11:36 UTC. Changes 50 and 51 come from the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install two of its sections, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 52 to 57 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>records.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with the landing, and with two findings of the batch&rsquo;s review on how the workers worked: a shallow clone, and a timed run that measured one commit twice. The landing also changed the passage of change 43, which now shows it, with a second reason, &ldquo;Why, climb batch 28&rdquo;. Changes 58 to 67 come from cuts. <code>compiler.md</code> had grown to 30,491 characters, past 30,000, the size past which a worker&rsquo;s <code>cat</code> of the part is saved to a file and read again, as two agents of the batch had to do. To bring it under, the skill writer cut text that states no rule, and it is now 29,999 characters. Change 58 defines a term whose pointer a cut took away, and changes 59 to 67 are the cuts. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed all of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
175	<p>Five items of climb batch 27 are new rules: change 39, a type without its own <code>=</code> compares by strict equivalence; change 40, the static argument written where walk's printed type misleads; change 41, <code>Number</code> declares nothing; change 42, walk's float numerals under strict equivalence; change 48, a commit already made whose footer names a model is left as it is. The other five from that batch state facts that it changed, and change no rule. Change 49 is not a new rule either: it defines two terms. Nor are the changes of 8 November to change 39: it now spells out strict equivalence and three cases where a worker would guess wrong.</p>
176	<p>Of climb batch 28&rsquo;s items, two are new rules: change 51, how to convert an integer into another integer type; change 55, what to do in a shallow clone. One changes a rule: change 56, where the count stage&rsquo;s run after the second pass is now skipped when that pass changed no code. The other fifteen, and the change to change 43, state facts that the batch changed, define a term, fix a word or a citation, or cut text that states no rule.</p>
177	<p>Each item shows the skill&rsquo;s current text, as it stands on <code>main</code>, against the text before the item&rsquo;s first commit. When a later commit changes an item&rsquo;s passage, the change is folded into that item, with its own reason, and does not become a new item. The words that came in are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. The next new change will be change 68.</p>
